    "transcript": [
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "Good afternoon. In today's lecture, I'm going to talk about plastic recycling: what happens to plastic after you put it in the recycling bin. Many people think that all plastic can be recycled, but that's not true."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "Let's start with the numbers. Every year, the world produces about three hundred and fifty million tonnes of plastic. Only around nine per cent of it is recycled. Most of the rest goes to landfill, is burned, or ends up in the environment, including the oceans."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "So, how does recycling work? There are four main steps. First, the plastic is collected from homes and businesses. Second, it is sorted by type. This is done partly by machines and partly by people, and it's one of the hardest steps, because there are many kinds of plastic and they must not be mixed. Third, the plastic is washed to remove food and labels. Finally, it is melted and made into small pieces called pellets, which factories use to make new products."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "Not every product can be recycled in the same way. Plastic bottles are the easiest. They can become new bottles, clothing or even carpets. For example, about twenty-five bottles are needed to make one fleece jacket. On the other hand, thin plastic bags and food wrappers are much harder to recycle, because they are light and often dirty. Many recycling centres do not accept them."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "There are also problems with quality. Each time plastic is recycled, it becomes a little weaker, so it can usually be recycled only two or three times. Because of this, some scientists argue that we should focus on using less plastic, not only on recycling it."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "Next week, we'll look at new materials that might replace plastic, such as seaweed packaging. Before then, please read the article in your course book and prepare two questions to ask in class."
      }
    ]
  },
